Q: Nginx limit upload speed A: <p>While credit for the core approach goes to @Spring (thank you), the configuration provided should be augmented with a few additional directives (specifically <code>proxy_ssl_name</code> and <code>proxy_ssl_server_name</code>) if you are proxying requests to an external HTTPS service.</p>
<p>Below is an example of a CORS proxy configuration for the JSFiddle <a href="https://docs.jsfiddle.net/async-requests" rel="nofollow noreferrer">echo services</a>, featuring both upload and download throttling:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>stream {
    server {
        listen 8443;
        proxy_upload_rate 1m;
        proxy_download_rate 1m;
        proxy_pass jsfiddle.net:443;
    }
}

http {
    ...
    server {
        ...
        location / {
            ...
        }
        location /echo/ {
            client_max_body_size 10m;
            proxy_request_buffering off;
            proxy_buffering off;
            proxy_set_header Host jsfiddle.net;
            proxy_ssl_name jsfiddle.net;
            proxy_ssl_server_name on;
            proxy_pass https://127.0.0.1:8443;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you need to introduce a second proxying layer in a production environment, consider listening on a UNIX socket rather than TCP port to eliminate network stack overhead:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>stream {
    server {
        listen unix:/var/run/nginx-stream.sock;
        ...
    }
}

http {
    ...
    server {
        ...
        location = /upload {
            ...
            proxy_pass http://unix:/var/run/nginx-stream.sock;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
